tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Contributions.}
We present an empirical case study of Goodhart collapse in solver-backed RL, using FOLIO with Qwen3 (4B/1.7B) across 6 reward conditions, 5 seeds, 2 logic types, and up to 1000 steps:
(\textbf{C1}) ISR as a diagnostic for GRPO reward hacking, revealing a 99.5\% structural collapse invisible to prove rate, with an SFT control consistent with (though not conclusive of) RL-specific failure (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic});
(\textbf{C2}) an empirical characterization of Goodhart collapse dynamics under LOO-based rewards, including seed-dependent divergence, dose-varying patterns, and a hacking taxonomy of eight automated-classifier modes plus one manual-audit mode (H9) (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}--\ref{sec:taxonomy});
(\textbf{C3}) cross-logic extension to Prolog with consistent Goodhart effects across all seeds (\S\ref{sec:crosslogic}).
These are cautionary results from a single model family and dataset: snapshot evaluation of structural rewards can overestimate their benefit; only trajectory analysis reveals the transient nature of apparent improvement.

\paragraph{Dose-response coverage.}
We test four $\beta$ values ($0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3$) with 3--5 seeds per dose.
This sparse sampling is insufficient to characterize the dose-response surface or identify critical thresholds; the qualitative differences across doses (deterministic collapse at $\beta{=}0.05/0.2$, seed-dependent divergence at $\beta{=}0.1$, multi-modal instability at $\beta{=}0.3$) should be treated as preliminary observations, not a systematic dose-response relationship.

\paragraph{ISR computation overhead.}
STA requires $O(n)$ solver calls per sample, where $n$ is the component count.
For our experiments (mean $n{\approx}6$--$8$), this adds ${\sim}6$--$8{\times}$ overhead per proved sample during evaluation; at training time with ISR reward, the overhead applies to all proved rollouts per GRPO step.

\paragraph{Multiple comparisons.}
We report individual $p$-values without correction for multiple comparisons across conditions, doses, and metrics.
Given the exploratory nature of the dose-response analysis and the limited seed counts, readers should interpret reported $p$-values as descriptive rather than confirmatory.

\paragraph{Component decomposition edge cases.}

Applying STA to richer logics (e.g., higher-order, temporal) may require domain-specific component decomposition strategies.

\paragraph{Concurrent and complementary diagnostics.}
TRACE~\citep{wang2025trace} detects reward hacking by measuring reasoning effort (truncated chain-of-thought AUC), while STA measures structural necessity (component ablation).
These approaches are complementary: TRACE captures effort-based shortcuts in chain-of-thought reasoning, whereas STA captures structural shortcuts in solver-backed formal outputs.
Combining both diagnostic axes (effort and necessity) may provide more robust hacking detection than either alone.

